{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012AboZSK1JwAJAuCM1sMiKL","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a clear picture. Let me create a comprehensive analysis document:\n\n## Comprehensive Analysis: GPU Performance Bottleneck in STARK Prover Stacked Reduction\n\nBased on my thorough code analysis, here's the complete picture of the stacked reduction orchestration and performance bottleneck:\n\n### **1. HOW STACKED REDUCTION IS ORCHESTRATED (Per AIR Instance Model)**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` (lines 184-269, 294-316)\n\nThe orchestration is **strictly per AIR instance**, not batched:\n\n```\nfor (air_idx, air_ctx) in ctx.per_trace.iter() {\n    // Each AIR instance contributes:\n    // 1. Common main traces (commit_idx=0, all AIRs share this)\n    // 2. Per-AIR preprocessed data (one commit per AIR)\n    // 3. Per-AIR cached main traces (one commit per AIR)\n}\n```\n\nEach AIR instance can have **multiple commits** (common main + preprocessed + cached), and each commit contains multiple **unstacked traces** grouped by height.\n\n**Key data structure:** `ht_diff_idxs` (height-diff-indices) at `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` lines 80, 351-379:\n- Marks boundaries where trace heights change within the unstacked columns\n- Creates \"windows\" of columns with the same height\n- Lines 393-397: `max_window_len = ht_diff_idxs.windows(2).map(|w| w[1] - w[0]).max()`\n\n**Processing model (lines 508-552):**\n```rust\nfor ((trace_ptr, trace_height, trace_width), window) in zip(\n    self.trace_ptrs,           // One entry per AIR instance's per-commit matrix\n    self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)  // Height-based groupings\n) {\n    // ONE kernel launch per trace/AIR instance per round\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. WHAT CAUSES 17,018+ KERNEL LAUNCHES (APC 300 case)**\n\n**Root cause:** Independent per-trace kernel launches across rounds 1..=n_stack\n\n**Calculation for APC 300:**\n- `ht_diff_idxs.len() - 1` = number of distinct height groups = ~1,000+ windows\n- Each window launches **one kernel per round** (rounds 1..=n_stack)\n- With n_stack ≈ 17: **~17,000 total kernel launches**\n\n**Documented at:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` lines 832-901\n\n```rust\nlet mut s_evals_batch = Vec::with_capacity(self.ht_diff_idxs.len() - 1);\nfor window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2) {\n    let window_len = window[1] - window[0];\n    \n    if log_height < l_skip + round {\n        // DEGENERATE: grid(1, min(window_len, 256))\n        stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate(...)\n    } else {\n        // NON-DEGENERATE: grid(num_y, tuned_stride), num_y = 1 << (log_height - l_skip - round)\n        stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round(...)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. THE \"DEGENERATE\" KERNEL CALLS - 10,348 INSTANCES**\n\n**Definition:** Degenerate case occurs when `log_height < l_skip + round`\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` lines 848-878\n\nWhen this condition holds:\n- The sumcheck polynomial evaluations become **constants** (no interpolation needed)\n- The `eq_r` and `k_rot_r` values are **pre-computed and stable** (stored in `eq_stable`, `k_rot_stable`)\n- This is **always true for all n < 0 cases** (shorter traces)\n\n**Kernel launch parameters (degenerate):**\n- **File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` lines 537-570\n- Grid: `(1, 1)` - **Single block only!**\n- Block: `min(window_len, 256)`\n- This is the **critical bottleneck**\n\n**Why 10,348 degenerate calls?**\n- APC 300: 623 AIR instances × (some with log_height < l_skip + round for multiple rounds)\n- For each round r in [1, n_stack]:\n  - Any trace with log_height < l_skip + r triggers degenerate path\n  - With n_stack ≈ 17, shorter traces are degenerate across most rounds\n\n**Degenerate kernel code (lines 306-368):**\n```cuda\n__global__ void stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel(\n    ...\n) {\n    FpExt local_sums[S_DEG];  // Per-thread accumulator\n    \n    // SINGLE BLOCK: all threads cooperate\n    for (uint32_t window_idx = threadIdx.x; window_idx < window_len; window_idx += blockDim.x) {\n        // Process one column per thread iteration\n        // Read q_0, q_1 from specific row (determined by shift_factor = l_skip + round)\n        // Compute contribution with fixed eq_r, k_rot_r\n    }\n    \n    // Block-wide reduction to single output\n    FpExt reduced = sumcheck::block_reduce_sum(local_sums[idx], shared);\n    if (threadIdx.x == 0) {\n        sumcheck::atomic_add_fpext_to_u64(output + idx * 4, reduced);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Performance issue:**\n- Grid.x = 1: **Cannot parallelize across multiple column-groups**\n- Window size = unstacked columns in height group = potentially 1-4 columns for small traces\n- With grid(1, 1) and block(1-256): **Severe underutilization** on modern GPUs (80+ SMs)\n\n---\n\n### **4. NON-DEGENERATE KERNEL LAUNCHES & AUTO-TUNING**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` lines 493-535\n\nGrid parameter auto-tuning (lines 509-524):\n```cuda\nconstexpr uint32_t WAVES_TARGET = 4;    // blocks/SM target\nconstexpr uint32_t ITERS_MIN = 4;       // prefer >= 4 window iters/thread\nconstexpr uint32_t ITERS_MAX = 16;      // prefer <= 16 window iters/thread\n\nuint32_t stride_occ = div_ceil(sm_count * WAVES_TARGET, grid.x);\nuint32_t stride_loop_lo = div_ceil(window_len, ITERS_MAX);\nuint32_t stride_loop_hi = div_ceil(window_len, ITERS_MIN);\n\nuint32_t lo = std::max(1u, std::max(stride_occ, stride_loop_lo));\nuint32_t hi = std::min(std::min(window_len, MAX_GRID_DIM), stride_loop_hi);\nuint32_t tuned_stride = (lo <= hi) ? lo : std::min(lo, std::min(window_len, MAX_GRID_DIM));\ngrid.y = tuned_stride;  // Window stride\n```\n\n- Grid.x = ceil(num_y / 256) — can be reasonable\n- Grid.y = auto-tuned window stride — **still one kernel per height group per round**\n\n---\n\n### **5. THE ROOT PROBLEM: PER-HEIGHT-GROUP KERNEL LAUNCHES**\n\n**Architectural issue at:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` lines 833-901\n\n**Current model:**\n```rust\nfor window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2) {  // Iterate height groups\n    // One kernel launch per height group per round\n    if degenerate {\n        launch grid(1, 1)\n    } else {\n        launch grid(ceil(num_y/256), stride)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Why this degrades with more AIR instances:**\n1. More AIR instances → more distinct heights → more boundaries in ht_diff_idxs\n2. More height groups → more separate kernel launches\n3. Each kernel has **small grid.x** for degenerate cases or **varying num_y** for non-degenerate\n4. **GPU cannot amortize kernel launch overhead** across more data\n\n**Example scaling:**\n- 99 AIR instances: ~1,000 height groups → ~17,000 total kernels\n- 623 AIR instances: ~10,000+ height groups → 170,000+ total kernels\n\n---\n\n### **6. KEY FILE LOCATIONS & CRITICAL SECTIONS**\n\n| Component | File | Lines | Key Insight |\n|-----------|------|-------|-------------|\n| **Orchestration logic** | `stacked_reduction.rs` | 832-901 | Per-height-group kernel launches |\n| **Degenerate launcher** | `stacked_reduction.cu` | 537-570 | Grid(1,1) - BOTTLENECK |\n| **Degenerate kernel** | `stacked_reduction.cu` | 306-368 | Single block processes all columns |\n| **Non-degenerate launcher** | `stacked_reduction.cu` | 493-535 | Auto-tuned grid.y but still per-window |\n| **Non-degenerate kernel** | `stacked_reduction.cu` | 228-302 | Grid.x parallelizes num_y, grid.y strides window |\n| **Height grouping setup** | `stacked_reduction.rs` | 351-379 | ht_diff_idxs created per trace boundary |\n| **Max window calculation** | `stacked_reduction.rs` | 393-397 | Determines d_eq_ub buffer size |\n\n---\n\n### **7. WHY PERFORMANCE DEGRADES: 0.11s → 0.31s**\n\n**Breakdown:**\n\n1. **Kernel launch overhead dominates:**\n   - 17,018 launches × ~1-5 µs per launch = **17-85 ms overhead** (underestimate)\n   - GPU execution < 0.31s total suggests launches consume **30-50%** of time\n\n2. **Degenerate kernels have terrible utilization:**\n   - Grid(1, 1) with 80+ SMs: **98.75% SM underutilization**\n   - Block size = min(window_len, 256) often ≤ 256 threads\n   - For window_len = 1-4: **Only 1-4 threads per block!**\n   - Occupancy = 1-4 threads / 1024 = **0.1-0.4%**\n\n3. **Memory bandwidth underutilization:**\n   - Each degenerate launch reads O(window_len) columns\n   - Atomic writes serialize across warps\n   - Small grids cannot hide memory latency\n\n4. **Scaling effect (99 → 623 AIR instances):**\n   - Window count increases ~6.2×\n   - Total kernels increase ~6.2×\n   - Per-kernel underutilization stays constant BUT total overhead balloons\n   - 0.11s × 6.2 ≈ **0.68s (expected) vs 0.31s (observed)** — suggests worse-than-linear degradation or partial utilization\n\n---\n\n### **8. OPTIMIZATION OPPORTUNITIES**\n\n**To batch multiple height groups into fewer kernels:**\n\n1. **Degenerate path optimization:**\n   - Change launcher grid to `(num_height_groups, min(window_len, 256))`\n   - Process all degenerate height groups in **single coordinated kernel**\n   - Each block handles one height group, reads its columns\n\n2. **Unified launcher (degenerate + non-degenerate):**\n   - Distinguish by `eq_r, k_rot_r` (constants) vs `eq_r_ns, k_rot_ns` (segments)\n   - Merge into single kernel with conditional branching\n   - Reduce launch overhead by ~10×\n\n3. **Window merging:**\n   - Allow multiple height groups to be processed by same kernel\n   - Use shared memory for per-height intermediate reductions\n   - Requires careful handling of different `log_height` per group\n\n4. **Dynamic kernel serialization:**\n   - For degenerate kernels with window_len < 100: merge into **batched