{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjPgKk9bzHj1HCYb4g3WAg","type":"tool_result","content":"# OpenVM v2 Documentation Summary\n\n## Overview\nThis page documents OpenVM v2, a GPU-optimized proving system with experimental results for cryptographic operations.\n\n## Key Resources\n\n**Research Paper:**\nThe foundational work is available at openvm.dev/swirl.pdf\n\n**Core Experiments:**\nThe documentation presents comparative analysis across two computational architectures—GPU and CPU implementations—for two critical cryptographic primitives.\n\n## GPU Experiments\n\n**Keccak Hash Function:**\n- Performance metrics tracked via interactive visualization dashboard\n- Automatic precompile analysis comparing baseline (apc000) against manual optimization approaches\n\n**Pairing Operations:**\n- Similar dual-analysis structure with metrics comparison\n- Baseline (apc000) compared against apc300 optimization variant\n\n## CPU Experiments\n\n**Keccak Hash Function:**\n- Metrics evaluated against apc000 baseline\n- Separate precompile candidate analysis included\n\n**Pairing Operations:**\n- Multi-variant comparison (apc000 vs apc300)\n- Timestamped analysis (01_04 iterations)\n\n## Technical Resources\n\n**GPU Development:**\nNVIDIA's ecosystem tools are referenced, including Nsight Systems and Nsight Compute profilers for performance analysis.\n\n**Educational Material:**\nCUDA programming documentation and instructional video content support implementation understanding.\n\nAll experiments use gist-hosted JSON data for reproducible analysis through powdr-labs' specialized visualization platforms."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f2238e8-ef27-4ab7-979f-317ec3807970","uuid":"b2011805-fa87-4be0-a7ef-cdde0ecbf59c","timestamp":"2026-04-10T17:29:21.384Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":2553,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# OpenVM v2 Documentation Summary\n\n## Overview\nThis page documents OpenVM v2, a GPU-optimized proving system with experimental results for cryptographic operations.\n\n## Key Resources\n\n**Research Paper:**\nThe foundational work is available at openvm.dev/swirl.pdf\n\n**Core Experiments:**\nThe documentation presents comparative analysis across two computational architectures—GPU and CPU implementations—for two critical cryptographic primitives.\n\n## GPU Experiments\n\n**Keccak Hash Function:**\n- Performance metrics tracked via interactive visualization dashboard\n- Automatic precompile analysis comparing baseline (apc000) against manual optimization approaches\n\n**Pairing Operations:**\n- Similar dual-analysis structure with metrics comparison\n- Baseline (apc000) compared against apc300 optimization variant\n\n## CPU Experiments\n\n**Keccak Hash Function:**\n- Metrics evaluated against apc000 baseline\n- Separate precompile candidate analysis included\n\n**Pairing Operations:**\n- Multi-variant comparison (apc000 vs apc300)\n- Timestamped analysis (01_04 iterations)\n\n## Technical Resources\n\n**GPU Development:**\nNVIDIA's ecosystem tools are referenced, including Nsight Systems and Nsight Compute profilers for performance analysis.\n\n**Educational Material:**\nCUDA programming documentation and instructional video content support implementation understanding.\n\nAll experiments use gist-hosted JSON data for reproducible analysis through powdr-labs' specialized visualization platforms.","durationMs":7274,"url":"https://gist.githubusercontent.com/leonardoalt/cb8fba32b08669fc63dea4d7ab0b67af/raw/openvm_v2.md"}}
