{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HoP7oVbdBhmzuvgiCpNzsb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SkdpRmjTU6u3S7weobaTPB","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Defer Round 0 D2H Synchronization\n\nTask name: 2026-04-10-1933-defer-round0-d2h-sync\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase is the single largest contributor to the GPU prover's failure to scale with APC count. As APCs increase from 0 to 300, AIR instances grow from 99 to 623, but Round 0 time *increases* from 178ms to 663ms (3.7x worse), despite total computational work (cells, constraint instances, bus interaction messages) all decreasing by >2x.\n\nProfiling data from nsight shows that APC 300 launches ~935 Round 0 kernels (`zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_*` + `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_*`), each followed by a **blocking D2H transfer and CPU-side processing** before the next kernel can launch. This sequential pattern is in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`.\n\nThe per-AIR loop structure is:\n```\nfor each AIR instance (623 total for APC 300):\n    1. Launch zerocheck kernel on GPU\n    2. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 792)\n    3. CPU-side IDFT computation\n    4. Launch logup kernel on GPU\n    5. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 848)\n    6. CPU-side polynomial construction\n```\n\nThis creates 1,246 blocking synchronization points. Between each sync, the GPU sits idle while the CPU processes results. The result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each — just a few extension field elements per coset evaluation), so the overhead comes entirely from the synchronization and pipeline stalls, not from data transfer volume.\n\nCritically, **all loop iterations are independent**: the zerocheck and logup kernels for AIR[i] do not depend on results from any other AIR. The only shared read-only data are `d_lambda_pows` and `eq_xi_tree`.\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nRestructure the Round 0 per-AIR loop in `LogupZerocheckGpu::batch_constraints_round0()` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`) into two phases:\n\n**Phase 1 — GPU Launch Phase**: Iterate over all AIR instances, launching both zerocheck and logup kernels for each, without any intermediate D2H transfers. Collect the GPU result buffers (`DeviceBuffer`) into vectors. The kernels execute sequentially on the default CUDA stream (which is correct and safe), but the CPU doesn't block waiting for each one.\n\n```rust\nlet mut zc_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<EF>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\nlet mut logup_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<FracExt>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\n\nfor (trace_idx, ...) in ctx.per_trace... {\n    // Same setup as before (single_pk, d_main_parts, etc.)\n    \n    let sum_buffer = evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu(...)?;\n    zc_results.push((trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...));\n    \n    let sum = evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu(...)?;\n    logup_results.push((trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...));\n}\n```\n\n**Phase 2 — CPU Processing Phase**: After all kernels are queued, process all results. The first `to_host()` call will implicitly synchronize the stream (waiting for all queued kernels to complete), and subsequent `to_host()` calls will be instant since the data is already computed.\n\n```rust\nfor (trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...) in zc_results {\n    if !sum_buffer.is_empty() {\n        let q_evals = sum_buffer.to_host()?;\n        // CPU IDFT and polynomial construction (unchanged)\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * num_present_airs + trace_idx] = ...;\n    }\n}\n\nfor (trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...) in logup_results {\n    if !sum.is_empty() {\n        let evals = sum.to_host()?;\n        // CPU polynomial construction (unchanged)\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx] = ...;\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx + 1] = ...;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey implementation details:\n- `d_main_parts` (per-AIR GPU pointer arrays) must be collected to outlive Phase 1\n- Result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each), so holding 623 simultaneously uses negligible GPU memory (~600KB total)\n- No CUDA kernel changes are needed — only Rust-side loop restructuring\n- The GPU still executes kernels sequentially on the default stream, but without host-side pipeline stalls between launches\n\nOptionally, the CPU processing in Phase 2 can be parallelized using rayon (`par_iter`) since each AIR's IDFT is independent, further reducing CPU-side time.\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **APC 300 Round 0**: 663ms -> ~350-450ms (35-47% reduction)\n  - Eliminating 1,246 sync points removes ~200-300ms of pipeline stall overhead\n  - Optionally parallelizing CPU IDFTs with rayon saves additional ~50-100ms\n- **APC 0 Round 0**: 178ms -> ~130-160ms (10-27% reduction, less impact due to fewer instances)\n- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: 2478ms -> ~2180-2280ms (8-12% improvement)\n- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace): improves from 1.15 toward ~1.05\n\nThis is the first step toward proper scaling. Subsequent optimizations (stacked reduction batching, multi-stream pipelining) can compound on this improvement.\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace for APC 300 decreases by at least 150ms (from 2478ms baseline)\n2. **Secondary**: Round 0 time for APC 300 decreases by at least 30% (from 663ms to <465ms)\n3. **Scaling**: The ratio (APC 300 STARK excl trace) / (APC 0 STARK excl trace) improves (currently 1.15, target < 1.10)\n4. **Correctness**: All existing tests pass, proof verification succeeds for all APC configurations\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Batch stacked reduction degenerate kernels** (ranked #2): Replace 10,348 single-block degenerate kernel launches with ~17 batched launches (one per round). Expected ~100-150ms savings on Stacked Reduction (0.31s -> ~0.16-0.20s). This is a good follow-up task but has lower impact (targets +195ms anti-scaling vs Round 0's +485ms) and requires CUDA kernel changes.\n\n2. **Multi-stream Round 0 with CPU/GPU overlap** (ranked #3): Use multiple CUDA streams to overlap GPU execution with CPU IDFT processing. Higher potential (~300-400ms savings) but significantly more complex — requires async D2H transfers, stream management, and event-based synchronization. Better suited as a follow-up after the simpler deferred-sync approach proves the benefit.\n\n3. **Fuse Round 0 kernels across AIR instances** (ranked #4): Launch a single large kernel that processes multiple AIRs simultaneously. High potential but difficult because each AIR has different constraint structures (different proving keys, different numbers of constraints and interactions). The data-driven kernel would be complex to implement correctly.\n\n4. **Parallelize CPU IDFT with rayon** (ranked #5 standalone, but can be combined with #1): Wrap the CPU-side IDFT processing in `rayon::par_iter`. Simple and effective (~50-100ms savings) but smaller standalone impact. Included as an optional enhancement in the proposed approach.\n\nThe deferred D2H sync approach was chosen over these alternatives because it provides the highest (impact x probability / complexity) ratio: it targets the largest bottleneck (+485ms), requires only Rust-side changes (no CUDA kernel modifications), has minimal risk (loop iterations are provably independent), and establishes the foundation for future streaming optimizations.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":969,"cache_read_input_tokens":94585,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":969},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f2238e8-ef27-4ab7-979f-317ec3807970","uuid":"2563c3b1-07c8-48c2-96b0-f61f7ac2e814"}
