sfer volume.\n\nCritically, **all loop iterations are independent**: the zerocheck and logup kernels for AIR[i] do not depend on results from any other AIR. The only shared read-only data are `d_lambda_pows` and `eq_xi_tree`.\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nRestructure the Round 0 per-AIR loop in `LogupZerocheckGpu::batch_constraints_round0()` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`) into two phases:\n\n**Phase 1 — GPU Launch Phase**: Iterate over all AIR instances, launching both zerocheck and logup kernels for each, without any intermediate D2H transfers. Collect the GPU result buffers (`DeviceBuffer`) into vectors. The kernels execute sequentially on the default CUDA stream (which is correct and safe), but the CPU doesn't block waiting for each one.\n\n```rust\nlet mut zc_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<EF>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\nlet mut logup_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<FracExt>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\n\nfor (trace_idx, ...) in ctx.per_trace... {\n    // Same setup as before (single_pk, d_main_parts, etc.)\n    \n    let sum_buffer = evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu(...)?;\n    zc_results.push((trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...));\n    \n    let sum = evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu(...)?;\n    logup_results.push((trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...));\n}\n```\n\n**Phase 2 — CPU Processing Phase**: After all kernels are queued, process all results. The first `to_host()` call will implicitly synchronize the stream (waiting for all queued kernels to complete), and subsequent `to_host()` calls will be instant since the data is already computed.\n\n```rust\nfor (trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...) in zc_results {\n    if !sum_buffer.is_empty() {\n        let q_evals = sum_buffer.to_host()?;\n        // CPU IDFT and polynomial construction (unchanged)\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * num_present_airs + trace_idx] = ...;\n    }\n}\n\nfor (trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...) in logup_results {\n    if !sum.is_empty() {\n        let evals = sum.to_host()?;\n        // CPU polynomial construction (unchanged)\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx] = ...;\n        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx + 1] = ...;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey implementation details:\n- `d_main_parts` (per-AIR GPU pointer arrays) must be collected to outlive Phase 1\n- Result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each), so holding 623 simultaneously uses negligible GPU memory (~600KB total)\n- No CUDA kernel changes are needed — only Rust-side loop restructuring\n- The GPU still executes kernels sequentially on the default stream, but without host-side pipeline stalls between launches\n\nOptionally, the CPU processing in Phase 2 can be parallelized using rayon (`par_iter`) since each AIR's IDFT is independent, further reducing CPU-side time.\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **APC 300 Round 0**: 663ms -> ~350-450ms (35-47% reduction)\n  - Eliminating 1,246 sync points removes ~200-300ms of pipeline stall overhead\n  - Optionally parallelizing CPU IDFTs with rayon saves additional ~50-100ms\n- **APC 0 Round 0**: 178ms -> ~130-160ms (10-27% reduction, less impact due to fewer instances)\n- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: 2478ms -> ~2180-2280ms (8-12% improvement)\n- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace): improves from 1.15 toward ~1.05\n\nThis is the first step toward proper scaling. Subsequent optimizations (stacked reduction batching, multi-stream pipelining) can compound on this improvement.\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace for APC 300 decreases by at least 150ms (from 2478ms baseline)\n2. **Secondary**: Round 0 time for APC 300 decreases by at least 30% (from 663ms to <465ms)\n3. **Scaling**: The ratio (APC 300 STARK excl trace) / (APC 0 STARK excl trace) improves (currently 1.15, target < 1.10)\n4. **Correctness**: All existing tests pass, proof verification succeeds for all APC configurations\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Batch stacked reduction degenerate kernels** (ranked #2): Replace 10,34